    <section class="what-is-section">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="what-is-grid">
                <div class="what-is-content"> <span class="section-label">ELECTROMAGNETIC CRACK DETECTION</span>
                    <h2>What is Alternating Current Field Measurement (ACFM)?</h2>
                    <p class="lead">Alternating Current Field Measurement (ACFM) is an advanced electromagnetic inspection technique that detects and sizes surface-breaking and near-surface cracks by measuring distortions in an induced uniform electromagnetic field, enabling crack detection through protective coatings, paint, marine growth, and corrosion products without any surface preparation or coating removal.</p>
                    <p>ACFM operates by inducing a uniform alternating current (AC) electromagnetic field in the component using a transmit coil energized at frequencies typically between 1-10 kHz. In defect-free material, this field flows uniformly parallel to the surface. When a crack is present, the electromagnetic field is forced to flow around and beneath the crack, causing measurable distortions in the field pattern. An array of sensor coils positioned above the surface detects these field perturbations in two orthogonal directions (Bx and Bz components).</p>
                    <p>The unique butterfly-shaped field distortion signature is characteristic of cracks—enabling differentiation from benign features like geometric discontinuities, material property variations, or surface roughness. Advanced signal processing algorithms analyze the Bx component (parallel to surface) to determine crack length, and the Bz component (perpendicular to surface) to calculate crack depth. ACFM provides quantitative sizing: depth accuracy ±0.5mm or ±10% (whichever greater), length accuracy ±2mm for cracks 5-100mm long. Critical advantage: inspection through non-conductive coatings up to 6mm thick eliminates costly and time-consuming coating removal, surface grinding, and post-inspection recoating—reducing inspection costs by 70-90% in offshore and marine applications.</p>
                    <div class="feature-list">
                        <div class="feature-item"> <i class="fas fa-check-circle"></i>
                            <div> <strong>No Surface Preparation</strong>
                                <p>Inspect as-found surfaces without grinding, blasting, paint removal, or cleaning—saving time and cost</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="feature-item"> <i class="fas fa-check-circle"></i>
                            <div> <strong>Through-Coating Capability</strong>
                                <p>Penetrates paint, epoxy, fireproofing, rubber, and marine fouling up to 6mm thickness</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="feature-item"> <i class="fas fa-check-circle"></i>
                            <div> <strong>Quantitative Crack Sizing</strong>
                                <p>Measures both depth and length for engineering critical assessment (ECA) and fitness-for-service</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="feature-item"> <i class="fas fa-check-circle"></i>
                            <div> <strong>Fatigue Crack Monitoring</strong>
                                <p>Baseline and periodic re-inspection tracks crack growth rates for remaining life prediction</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
