                                        <p>Commercial vessel drydocking for hull inspection and repair costs $500K-3M depending on vessel size (drydock rental, tug services, lost charter revenue 10-20 days). Classification society surveys traditionally require drydocking every 5 years (special survey) plus 2.5-year intermediate surveys. ACFM in-water hull inspection with diver or ROV deployment enables: (1) Pre-drydock defect mapping identifying repair zones for efficient drydock planning (minimizing drydock duration), (2) In-water crack monitoring extending drydock intervals from 5 to 7.5 years where classification society accepts ACFM data, (3) Targeted drydock versus full bottom blasting and coating (partial coating removal only at crack locations). Fleet operators report 30-40% reduction in drydock frequency and 50-60% reduction in coating work scope using ACFM-guided strategies—saving $1M-5M per vessel over 20-year service life. Naval fleets use ACFM for between-deployment inspections maintaining operational readiness without shipyard availability constraints.</p>
