    <section class="procedure-section">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="section-header center"> <span class="section-label">INSPECTION WORKFLOW</span>
                <h2>ACFM Inspection Procedure</h2>
                <p>Systematic approach ensuring accurate crack detection and sizing per industry standards</p>
            </div>
            <div class="procedure-timeline">
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">1</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Pre-Inspection Engineering Review</h3>
                        <p>Review structural drawings, stress analysis reports, and prior inspection records identifying high-risk locations: weld toes at stress concentrations (tubular joint hot spots per DNVGL-RP-C203), areas with historical cracking, high-cycle fatigue zones (vibration, wave loading), and regions with corrosion damage (pitting, general loss). Define inspection scope, acceptance criteria (crack size limits per BS 7910 or API 579 ECA), and ACFM probe selection (pencil probe for manual spot checks, array probe for automated weld scanning). Coordinate access logistics: rope access rigging points, scaffolding requirements, ROV/diver operations for subsea, or confined space entry permits.</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Inspection Plan with High-Risk Zone Map </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">2</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Access & Surface Assessment</h3>
                        <p>Establish safe access to inspection zones: rope access technicians position at weld locations, scaffolding erected for large-scale campaigns, or ROV deployed for subsea structures. Perform visual assessment documenting coating condition, corrosion extent, physical damage, and surface irregularities. ACFM requires NO surface preparation—inspect through existing coatings, paint, fireproofing, or marine fouling as-found. Only requirement: identify weld toe location for probe positioning (visible welds, weld profile detectable through thin coatings, or use reference drawings/weld maps). Measure coating thickness with electromagnetic gauge if >6mm (may exceed ACFM penetration capability requiring localized coating removal).</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Access Photos & Coating Condition Report </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">3</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>ACFM System Calibration</h3>
                        <p>Power on ACFM instrument and verify battery charge (8-12 hour runtime typical). Perform lift-off calibration: measure signal in air (no metal), then on reference plate (defect-free steel with similar coating thickness to inspection target) establishing baseline field strength. Load crack sizing calibration algorithms validated on extensive crack libraries (>5000 natural and EDM notch cracks). Verify probe operation: position on calibration block with known crack (depth and length marked), acquire data, confirm software correctly identifies and sizes crack within tolerance (±0.5mm depth, ±2mm length). Set alarm thresholds for automated indication flagging during scanning.</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> ACFM Calibration Record with Reference Scans </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">4</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Systematic ACFM Scanning</h3>
                        <p>Position ACFM probe on weld toe (crack initiation location for fatigue) and traverse along full weld length at controlled speed (10-30 mm/sec manual scanning; automated scanners 50-100 mm/sec). Technician monitors real-time Bx and Bz signal displays—characteristic "butterfly" pattern indicates crack presence (Bx shows length, Bz shows depth). Mark crack locations with paint pen or GPS coordinates. Scan both sides of weld (toe on each side of weld centerline) for complete coverage. For tubular joints, scan all brace-to-chord intersection toes (typically 4-8 weld toes per K/Y/T joint). Repeat scans perpendicular to initial direction if crack orientation uncertain (ACFM most sensitive to cracks perpendicular to scan direction).</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Raw ACFM Data Files with Crack Indications </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">5</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Data Analysis & Crack Sizing</h3>
                        <p>Certified ACFM analyst reviews all scan data identifying true crack indications versus false calls (weld geometry, undercut, surface roughness can produce minor signals). For each crack indication, measure Bz peak-to-peak amplitude (correlates to depth via calibration curve) and Bx signal width (correlates to length). ACFM software automatically calculates: crack depth (mm), crack length (mm), crack location (distance from reference point), crack orientation (angle relative to weld). Generate crack inventory table listing all detected cracks with dimensions. Compare against acceptance criteria: subcritical cracks (acceptable for continued service), critical cracks (require repair), monitoring cracks (re-inspect to track growth). Statistical analysis: crack distribution, maximum/mean depths, crack density (cracks per meter weld).</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Crack Inventory with Sizes & Locations </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">6</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Engineering Critical Assessment (ECA)</h3>
                        <p>For structures with detected cracks, perform fitness-for-service evaluation per BS 7910, API 579-1, or DNVGL-RP-C203 using ACFM crack sizing data. Calculate critical crack size (acc) for through-thickness failure based on applied stresses, material fracture toughness, and safety factors. Compare ACFM-measured crack sizes against acc: if a < 0.5×acc, continue operation with monitoring; if 0.5×acc < a < 0.8×acc, detailed fracture mechanics analysis required; if a> 0.8×acc, immediate repair mandatory. Estimate remaining fatigue life using Paris Law crack growth equation: da/dN = C(ΔK)^m where ΔK = stress intensity factor range. Calculate inspection interval for crack growth monitoring ensuring re-inspection before critical size reached.</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> ECA Report with Fitness-for-Service Conclusions </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-number">7</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">
                        <h3>Final Reporting & Recommendations</h3>
                        <p>Comprehensive inspection report includes: executive summary, structure/equipment description, inspection scope (locations, weld lengths scanned), ACFM procedure and calibration, crack detection results (inventory table, annotated drawings/photos showing crack locations), crack sizing data with Bx/Bz plots, fitness-for-service assessment and repair recommendations, monitoring plan for subcritical cracks (re-inspection frequency, crack growth rate predictions), and repair procedure guidance for critical cracks (weld prep, consumables, PWHT requirements). Digital deliverables: ACFM raw data files, crack location GPS coordinates (KML for GIS integration), comparison with prior inspections if available (crack growth trending). Report certified by ACFM Level 2/3 per PCN or ASNT guidelines.</p>
                        <div class="timeline-deliverable"> <strong>Deliverable:</strong> Final ACFM Inspection Report with Digital Data </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
