    <section class="faq-section">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="section-header center"> <span class="section-label">FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS</span>
                <h2>ACFM Inspection Questions Answered</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-container">
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>How does ACFM detect cracks through paint and coatings?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>ACFM uses low-frequency (1-10 kHz) alternating current electromagnetic fields that readily penetrate non-conductive materials like paint, epoxy, rubber, and fireproofing without significant attenuation. The electromagnetic field is induced in the electrically conductive steel substrate beneath the coating. When a crack is present, the field flows around and under the crack creating measurable distortions detected by sensor coils positioned above the coating surface. Non-conductive coatings (most protective coatings are non-conductive or very low conductivity) are essentially "transparent" to ACFM electromagnetic fields. Maximum practical coating thickness: 6mm due to sensor lift-off effects reducing signal strength, but most industrial coatings are 0.5-3mm well within ACFM capability. This eliminates need for costly coating removal (grit blasting), inspection, and recoating—saving $50K-500K per inspection zone on offshore platforms and refineries.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>Can ACFM work on stainless steel or aluminum?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>ACFM is optimized for ferromagnetic materials (carbon steel, low alloy steel, ferritic stainless steels like 409, 410, 430) where electromagnetic field penetration and crack-induced field distortions are most pronounced. For non-ferromagnetic materials (austenitic stainless steel 304/316, aluminum, titanium, copper alloys), ACFM capability is reduced but not entirely eliminated—detection possible for large/deep cracks but sensitivity significantly lower than on ferromagnetic materials. Alternative electromagnetic techniques for non-ferromagnetics: conventional eddy current testing (ECT) at higher frequencies (10-400 kHz) provides better performance. For crack detection on stainless/aluminum: ECT, PAUT (phased array ultrasonic), or liquid penetrant testing (PT) are typically preferred. ACFM's primary value is ferromagnetic material inspection with through-coating capability.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>How accurate is ACFM for measuring crack depth?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>ACFM provides ±0.5mm or ±10% crack depth accuracy (whichever is greater) based on extensive validation studies comparing ACFM sizing to destructive crack measurement (metallographic sectioning). For example: 3mm crack measured as 2.5-3.5mm; 10mm crack measured as 9-11mm. Accuracy depends on: crack orientation (perpendicular to scan direction optimal), crack shape (planar vs branched), coating thickness (reduced accuracy through thick coatings), and surface condition (roughness, corrosion). This accuracy is sufficient for engineering critical assessment (ECA) per BS 7910 or API 579-1 where conservative crack size assumptions and safety factors accommodate measurement uncertainty. For applications requiring higher precision (±0.1mm), ACFM is used for screening followed by TOFD (Time of Flight Diffraction ultrasonic) for precise through-wall sizing of critical cracks before repair decisions.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>Does the surface need to be smooth for ACFM?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>No, ACFM tolerates rough surfaces including weld toe geometry, undercut, minor spatter, and corrosion pitting significantly better than MPI or PT which require smooth, clean surfaces. ACFM probe design incorporates flexible housing conforming to surface contours and maintaining consistent lift-off (probe-to-surface distance) despite roughness. However, extreme surface irregularities can affect results: deep undercut (>2mm) may produce signals mimicking small cracks requiring analyst experience to differentiate; heavy weld spatter interfering with probe contact may require localized removal (wire brush, light grinding); severe corrosion pitting (>3mm depth, clustered) can mask underlying cracks requiring corrosion removal for verification. For typical painted structural welds with normal weld profile and minor surface imperfections, ACFM operates without additional surface preparation beyond as-found condition. This "inspect as-is" capability is ACFM's primary advantage over surface-sensitive techniques.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>Can ACFM detect cracks in all orientations?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>ACFM sensitivity varies with crack orientation relative to induced electromagnetic field direction. Maximum sensitivity: cracks perpendicular to field (and scan direction)—typical weld toe cracks along weld length detected by scanning perpendicular to weld. Reduced sensitivity: cracks parallel to field require scanning in orthogonal direction. For unknown crack orientation, scan in two perpendicular directions ensuring detection regardless of orientation. Branched or inclined cracks (not perpendicular to surface) produce weaker signals than planar surface-breaking cracks. ACFM detects cracks at stress concentrations (weld toes, geometric discontinuities) where crack orientation is usually predictable based on stress field—enabling optimized scan direction. For complex geometries or uncertain crack patterns, multi-directional scanning or complementary techniques (MPI after localized coating removal) provide verification. Offshore platform tubular joints scanned circumferentially and axially covering both expected fatigue crack orientations.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>How much does ACFM inspection cost compared to MPI?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>ACFM equipment cost per weld is higher than MPI ($300-800 vs $100-300) BUT total project cost including surface preparation is dramatically lower: ACFM offshore jacket node inspection through existing coating: $300-800 per node (probe, technician, reporting); Traditional MPI same node requiring coating removal: $2000-5000 (scaffolding access $500-1500, grit blasting $800-2000, MPI $100-300, recoating $600-1500, cure time/re-inspection). For 100-node offshore platform inspection campaign: ACFM total cost $30K-80K; Traditional MPI total cost $200K-500K. ACFM ROI exceeds 5:1 on coated structures. For bare steel fabrication QC where surface is already prepared, MPI may be more economical. ACFM value proposition: eliminate surface preparation and coating restoration costs (70-90% of total inspection budget on coated assets) while providing superior crack sizing data for engineering assessment versus qualitative MPI indications.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>Is ACFM accepted by classification societies and regulators?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes, ACFM is widely accepted by: Marine classification societies (ABS, DNV, Lloyd's Register, Bureau Veritas) for hull crack inspection and structural surveys; Offshore regulators (HSE UK, PSA Norway, BSEE US) for platform integrity management; Bridge and civil infrastructure authorities recognizing ACFM per AASHTO and FHWA guidelines; Oil & gas operators following API 579 / ASME FFS-1 for fitness-for-service assessment using ACFM crack sizing data. Specific standards recognizing ACFM: ISO 24497 (ACFM examination of welds), BS EN 17732 (ACFM general applications), ASTM E2884 (ACFM for crack detection/sizing). Many operators have qualified ACFM procedures per their internal standards demonstrating POD, sizing accuracy, and reliability through extensive trials. Classification societies may require ACFM + verification (MPI/UT on sample of indications) for initial acceptance, then ACFM-only for subsequent inspections after correlation established. ACFM gaining adoption as primary method due to cost savings and quantitative sizing enabling risk-based decision making.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        <h3>Can ACFM be used for crack growth monitoring?</h3> <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes, ACFM is ideal for crack growth monitoring due to: (1) Quantitative sizing repeatability (±0.5mm depth accuracy enables detection of 1-2mm growth between inspections), (2) No surface preparation required (same crack inspected multiple times without coating removal/restoration—eliminating surface condition variability affecting measurements), (3) Digital position referencing (GPS coordinates or distance from landmarks enable precise re-location of cracks for direct comparison), (4) Baseline + periodic re-inspection strategy (measure initial crack size, re-measure annually/biannually, calculate growth rate da/dt or da/dN). Typical offshore platform monitoring program: baseline ACFM inspection identifies 50-100 fatigue cracks (2-8mm depth) at high-stress nodes; annual ACFM re-inspection tracks growth; cracks showing >0.5mm/year growth rate flagged for accelerated monitoring or repair; stable cracks (no measurable growth) remain acceptable for continued service. Crack growth data feeds fracture mechanics remaining life calculations per BS 7910 or DNVGL-RP-C203 optimizing repair timing—proactive rather than reactive maintenance.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
